        "event_title":"CREES Noon Lecture. When Everyday Life Becomes an Event: Mobility in the Georgia-Abkhazia Conflict Zone",
        "occurrence_title":"",
        "combined_title":"CREES Noon Lecture. When Everyday Life Becomes an Event: Mobility in the Georgia-Abkhazia Conflict Zone: G\u00f6rkem Aydemir-Kundakci, Fonds Wetenschappelijk Onderzoek (FWO) Postdoctoral Fellow in Anthropology, Katholieke Universiteit Leuven, Belgium",
        "event_subtitle":"G\u00f6rkem Aydemir-Kundakci, Fonds Wetenschappelijk Onderzoek (FWO) Postdoctoral Fellow in Anthropology, Katholieke Universiteit Leuven, Belgium",
        "event_type":"Livestream \/ Virtual",
        "event_type_id":"24",
        "description":"What does it take to sustain an ordinary life in a place shaped by decades of unresolved conflict and political uncertainty? Drawing on long-term ethnographic research in the de facto Georgia-Abkhazia borderland, this talk examines how displaced Georgians with constructed lives across an unrecognized but highly militarized border learn to navigate a landscape in which mobility is continually subject to political, material, and documentary contingencies. Rather than approaching protracted conflict primarily through rupture, destruction, or spectacular violence, Aydemir-Kundakci focuses on everyday eventfulness: how ordinary journeys to attend a wedding, shop, visit family, or go to a hospital can unexpectedly become consequential\u2014when rising waters turn a familiar river crossing into a life-threatening swim, a document check evolves into harassment or extortion, or a passport stamp creates the risk of punishment on the other side.\n\nAydemir-Kundakci develops the concept of manageable eventfulness to describe the practical and anticipatory work through which people seek not necessarily to eliminate such contingencies altogether, but to keep their consequences within manageable limits. Following these practices across changing border regimes, landscapes, documents, and generations, she shows how people continually recalibrate their movements and anticipations as they strive for uneventful, ordinary lives amid prolonged conflict.\n\nDr. G\u00f6rkem Aydemir-Kundakci is an FWO Postdoctoral Fellow in Anthropology at KU Leuven. Her research examines long-term displacement, conflict, mobility, environmental change, and precarity in Georgia\u2019s de facto borderland with Abkhazia.\u202fShe holds a PhD in Anthropology from George Washington University and held a postdoctoral fellowship at the University of Calgary. Her writing has appeared in journals such as\u202fthe Journal of Refugee Studies, History and Anthropology, and Anthropology News. Aydemir-Kundakci\u2019s research has been funded by the National Science Foundation (NSF), American Council of Learned Societies (ACLS), and Research Foundation Flanders (FWO).\n\nPlease note this event will be held virtually on Zoom. Please register at this link: https:\/\/myumi.ch\/mVV9g\n\nIf there is anything we can do to make this event accessible to you, please contact us at CREES@umich.edu. Please be aware that advance notice is necessary as some accommodations may require more time for the university to arrange.\n\n*Part of the International Institute Theme Year: Sovereignty*\nhttps:\/\/myumi.ch\/ii-theme",
